"""
## Purpose and audience

This specification is maintained for compiler and library engineers reviewing UNIT-APP behavior, and for operators verifying a build.

## Operator workflow

Run `bin/simple test --no-session-daemon test/unit/app/replay/replay_no_self_spawn_spec.spl` and confirm the Results line reports 0 failed. Each scenario names the requirement it traces to and starts with a Verify step.

## Compatibility and limitations

Covers only the behavior explicitly asserted below on the current toolchain. No external standard or cross-version contract is claimed; numeric oracles are explained inline where they appear.

## Recovery and troubleshooting

If a scenario fails, re-run the single spec file, read the first failing Verify step, and compare the reported value against the inline oracle comment before bisecting recent changes.
"""
# @manual: primary
# REQ-UNIT-APP-001
# doc-path: doc/01_research/app/
# doc-path: doc/03_plan/app/
# doc-path: doc/04_architecture/app/
# doc-path: doc/05_design/app/
# @cover src/app/replay/main.spl 60%
"""
# CLI entrypoints must not re-invoke themselves

**Category:** Tooling
**Status:** Implemented

## Purpose and Audience

`simple replay <build-log>` once handled its unimplemented build-log branch by
re-invoking `./bin/simple replay <same args>`. Because the delegated child took
the same branch, it delegated again, forever: every process stayed alive blocked
in `wait`, producing ~124 live processes/min at ~62 MB RSS each (~8.4 GB/min)
until a 128 GB host was exhausted. `earlyoom` was configured with
`--prefer '^(simple|...)'`, so the resulting kills landed on *unrelated healthy*
`simple` processes -- Stage-3 bootstrap builds, test runs, MCP servers -- which
then presented as bare exit 143 with no diagnostic.

Bug: doc/08_tracking/bug/simple_replay_self_spawns_unbounded_process_chain_2026-08-10.md

## Scope and Preconditions

This is a SOURCE-INVARIANT spec. It deliberately does not execute the CLI: the
failure mode under test is an unbounded fork chain, and a spec that reproduced
it by running the binary would be the very host-exhaustion event it guards
against. Instead it reads the checked-in CLI entrypoint sources and asserts the
structural property whose absence caused the incident.

## Primary Workflow

An unimplemented (or not-found) CLI subcommand branch reports a terminating
diagnostic and returns a nonzero status. It never shells out to the same
subcommand of the same binary.

## Key Concepts

| Concept | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Self-spawn | A CLI branch invoking `bin/simple <its own subcommand>` |
| Terminating branch | Reports an error and returns; performs no delegation |

## Evidence and Provenance

Live re-verification 2026-08-17 with the exact incident argv:
`bin/simple replay missing-build-log.json` exits 1 printing
`log file not found: missing-build-log.json`, under a watchdog armed to kill at
>8 concurrent matching processes. The watchdog never fired and the process
count delta was 0.

## Recovery and Troubleshooting

If this spec fails, a CLI entrypoint has reintroduced self-delegation. Replace
the delegation with a terminating diagnostic; do not bound it with a depth
counter, which still multiplies processes.

## Compatibility and Limitations

Static/source-level. It cannot catch a self-spawn constructed dynamically from
a runtime-assembled command string.
"""

use std.spec.*
use std.fs.{read_file}


# ============================================================================
# Step Helpers
# ============================================================================

fn read_cli_source(path: text) -> text:
    read_file(path)

fn line_is_comment(line: text) -> bool:
    line.trim().starts_with("#")

fn spawns_own_subcommand(source: text, subcommand: text) -> bool:
    # A self-spawn is EXECUTABLE code naming the binary immediately followed by
    # the very subcommand this entrypoint implements. Comment lines are skipped
    # on purpose: the fixed `delegate_replay` carries a prose warning quoting
    # the old `./bin/simple replay <same args>` invocation, and a naive
    # substring scan would read that warning as the defect it warns about.
    #
    # It must ALSO be a process invocation. Naming the subcommand is not
    # enough: `print "Usage: simple replay [options] ..."` names it in a help
    # string and spawns nothing, so a name-only scan reports the usage banner
    # as a fork bomb.
    val needle = "simple " + subcommand
    val lines = source.split("\n")
    var i = 0
    while i < lines.len():
        val line = lines[i]
        if not line_is_comment(line) and line.contains(needle) and line_spawns(line):
            return true
        i = i + 1
    false

fn line_spawns(line: text) -> bool:
    line.contains("shell(") or line.contains("spawn") or
        line.contains("run_process") or line.contains("exec(") or
        line.contains("run_command")

fn expect_terminating_diagnostic(source: text, needle: text):
    expect(source.contains(needle)).to_equal(true)


# ============================================================================
# Primary Scenarios
# ============================================================================

describe "replay CLI never self-spawns":
    it "the build-log branch reports instead of delegating to itself":
        # @oracle: this scenario asserts through a file-local helper wrapping expect(<value>).to_equal(<expected>)
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-001
        step("Verify: the build-log branch reports instead of delegating to itself")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("Read the replay entrypoint source")
        val source = read_cli_source("src/app/replay/main.spl")
        step("The unimplemented build-log branch states so and stops")
        expect_terminating_diagnostic(source, "build-log replay is not yet implemented")
        step("A missing log file is a terminating error, not a delegation")
        expect_terminating_diagnostic(source, "log file not found")

    it "carries no invocation of its own subcommand":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-002
        step("Verify: carries no invocation of its own subcommand")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("Read the replay entrypoint source")
        val source = read_cli_source("src/app/replay/main.spl")
        step("No `bin/simple replay ...` self-invocation remains")
        expect(spawns_own_subcommand(source, "replay")).to_equal(false)

    it "keeps the incident rationale attached to the fixed branch":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-003
        step("Verify: keeps the incident rationale attached to the fixed branch")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("Read the replay entrypoint source")
        val source = read_cli_source("src/app/replay/main.spl")
        step("The bug record is cited where the self-spawn used to be")
        expect(source.contains("simple_replay_self_spawns_unbounded_process_chain")).to_equal(true)

    # Absence control: the detector must be capable of answering `true`, or the
    # three assertions above would pass against any source whatsoever.
    it "the self-spawn detector actually detects a self-spawn":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-004
        step("Verify: the self-spawn detector actually detects a self-spawn")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("Construct source that does delegate to its own subcommand")
        val bad = "fn delegate(): shell(\"./bin/simple replay \" + target)"
        step("The detector reports it")
        expect(spawns_own_subcommand(bad, "replay")).to_equal(true)
        step("And a same-shaped call to a DIFFERENT subcommand is not a self-spawn")
        expect(spawns_own_subcommand(bad, "build")).to_equal(false)
        step("A COMMENT quoting the old invocation is not a self-spawn")
        val commented = "# never do: shell(\"./bin/simple replay \" + target)"
        expect(spawns_own_subcommand(commented, "replay")).to_equal(false)
        step("A help/usage string naming the subcommand is not a self-spawn")
        val usage = "    print \"Usage: simple replay [options] <trace.srr>\""
        expect(spawns_own_subcommand(usage, "replay")).to_equal(false)


# ============================================================================
# Generalization: the property is not specific to `replay`
# ============================================================================

describe "no CLI entrypoint delegates to its own subcommand":
    it "holds for every entrypoint known to have had the hazard":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-005
        step("Verify: holds for every entrypoint known to have had the hazard")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("Check each entrypoint against its own subcommand name")
        val entries = [
            ["src/app/replay/main.spl", "replay"],
        ]
        var offenders: [text] = []
        var i = 0
        while i < entries.len():
            val path = entries[i][0]
            val subcommand = entries[i][1]
            val source = read_cli_source(path)
            if spawns_own_subcommand(source, subcommand):
                offenders.push(path)
            i = i + 1
        step("No entrypoint self-delegates")
        expect(offenders.len()).to_equal(0)  # oracle: expected value 0 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec
        step("The sweep examined a non-empty set (non-vacuity control)")
        expect(entries.len()).to_equal(1)  # oracle: expected value 1 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec
